-- Panel choice becomes guidance, and the research behind it gets a home.
--
-- /guides/choosing-panels shipped: why 3 x 400 W is not 2 x 600 W at the same
-- nameplate. Two new items and one update.
--
-- NOTE FOR WHOEVER APPLIES THIS. check:roadmap-sync reports two rows -- the
-- two battery-scraper items -- disagreeing between seed.sql and the live
-- board. That is NOT admin-UI drift and must not be "reconciled" here: the
-- 2026-09-09 review-gate work lives on feature/scraper-published-row-gate,
-- which is UNMERGED, and its three migrations (20260909000001-3) are ALREADY
-- APPLIED to the shared database. Those rows come back into agreement when
-- that branch merges, which also carries its own seed.sql update. Writing a
-- reconcile here would duplicate 20260909000002 and hide the real problem.
--
-- That same unmerged state is why `supabase db push --linked` refuses until
-- the branch lands: the remote has migration versions the local tree does not.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. The spec library learns what the scraping research found.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

update public.roadmap_items
  set description = 'Started 2026-08-30. src/lib/inverter-sizing.ts and src/lib/pv-string.ts carry INVERTER_PRESETS and PANEL_PRESETS behind an admission gate: every figure from the manufacturer''s own datasheet, opened and read, with a reachable sourceUrl and the matching review module passing. Four rows so far. Inverters: Sun Gold Power SPH8048P and SPH10048P (manufacturer manual V1.3 pp.58-59) and the EG4 6000XP (spec sheet VER 1.4.4). The EG4 row is the argument for the gate — refused on first attempt because its window and per-tracker current came from a search index, and when the PDF itself became readable the search figure proved WRONG: 25 A is the MAX. SHORT CIRCUIT INPUT CURRENT while MAX. USABLE INPUT CURRENT is 17 A. Recording 25 A as usable would have permitted about half again as many parallel strings as the tracker can convert, silently. That correction closed a modelling gap: pv-string.ts now treats current as a pair exactly like voltage, damage limit versus harvest limit. Panels: the Sun Gold Power SG550WM, from the manufacturer''s own datasheet served off their Shopify CDN (their own store, so it is their document, not a reseller''s transcription). A useful first row because it publishes all three fields the string maths needs and many sheets omit: the Voc coefficient, the Pmax coefficient, and the 25 A maximum series fuse rating. Its -0.35%/degC Voc coefficient is at the steep end, so it gains more voltage in cold than most: at a -25.9 degC design low it reaches 58.5 V against a 49.7 V label, taking eight in series on a 500 V input where the STC figure suggests ten. src/lib/panel-review.ts is the physics-shaped check, sibling of inverter-review.ts: Vmp below Voc, Imp below Isc, Pmax equal to Vmp x Imp within 5%, fill factor between 0.6 and 0.9, a NEGATIVE Voc coefficient, and a series fuse rating above Isc. SCRAPER FINDING, 2026-08-30: sungoldpower.com product pages DO carry an HTML spec table, but it omits the temperature coefficients and the maximum series fuse rating entirely — exactly the two protection-critical fields. An HTML-only scraper would emit rows that look complete and cannot size a string, silently falling back to a coefficient that understates cold Voc. A panel scraper therefore needs PDF text extraction, which is a devDependency decision not yet taken. Their product pages also cover more than one model in a single table (the 550 W page carries the SG450WM alongside it), so column selection needs the same hand verification the battery scrapers documented. Remaining: that scraper, Supabase panel_models and inverter_models tables with RLS, and /admin review queues mirroring /admin/batteries. Typing a datasheet in by hand stays a first-class path. SCRAPING RESEARCH, 2026-09-10 -- the decisions this item has been blocked on, taken. THE PDF-EXTRACTION DEPENDENCY IS DECIDABLE NOW: use unpdf or pdfjs-dist. Both are pure JavaScript with no native build step, which matters twice -- Homebrew is broken on the development machine, and production is going to Cloudflare, where a native binding would not run. pdf-parse wraps an old pdf.js and is less maintained. That single choice unblocks BOTH the panel and inverter scrapers, which is the whole remaining blocker recorded above. THE CEC SOLAR EQUIPMENT LIST IS THE LARGER UNLOCK, verified 2026-09-10: the California Energy Commission publishes a downloadable PV module list whose Full Data version carries reported nameplate ratings AND TEMPERATURE COEFFICIENTS across 300+ manufacturers -- exactly the fields the sungoldpower HTML table was found to omit. One file instead of N per-brand parsers. BUT IT COLLIDES WITH THE ADMISSION GATE, and that is a decision for the operator rather than a detail: the gate says every figure comes from the manufacturer''s OWN datasheet, opened and read, and a regulator''s list is a transcription. The argument for accepting it is that it is a STRONGER transcription than a reseller''s -- it is the value the manufacturer legally filed. PROPOSED MIDDLE PATH that keeps the gate intact: CEC rows land as unpublished candidates tagged source_tier = ''regulator'', and PUBLISHING still requires opening the manufacturer PDF. That buys a 300-brand candidate pool for free without weakening anything. NOT YET VERIFIED, do not plan around it: whether the Full Data list includes DIMENSIONS AND WEIGHT. Ratings and coefficients are confirmed; the physical fields are not. ALSO WORTH DOING: extend scrape-signaturesolar.ts and scrape-a1solarstore.ts to panels -- both scrapers already exist and are trusted, and both retailers sell panels, which is far cheaper than admitting a new brand. Santan Solar is worth a look for used and B-stock modules, the real DIY price floor, pending robots.txt and whether an affiliate programme exists. Renogy stays excluded on the existing robots.txt precedent. Freight class should be DERIVED from the longest dimension rather than scraped. AND ONE STRUCTURAL FINDING that belongs here: PanelSpec is electrical-only -- ten fields, all volts and amps. A panel_models table must carry the physical fields from the start or it will reproduce the same gap in the database that the TypeScript preset has today. See "Panel choice: form factor, granularity and the physical fields".'
  where title = 'Component spec library: inverters and panels';

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. The new items.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

insert into public.roadmap_items
  (phase, category, title, description, status, dev_percent_complete, is_public, display_order)
values
  (1, 'onboarding', 'Guide: choosing panels — why 3 x 400 W is not 2 x 600 W',
   'Shipped 2026-09-10 at /guides/choosing-panels. The half of "which panels?" that is not voltage, and a question the site had no answer to: two builds with IDENTICAL NAMEPLATE WATTS are not the same array. Every panel-buying guide stops at watts and price per watt, which serves a utility-scale buyer filling an empty field and is close to useless to someone putting an array on a cabin roof, a camper or a shed. Differentiator #1 applied to a buying decision instead of a term of art. EIGHT THINGS THAT DIFFER between 3 x 400 W and 2 x 600 W, both 1200 W. (1) AREA AND SHAPE. The SMALLER-panel build needs 13% MORE roof -- 5.86 m2 against 5.17 m2 -- because a bigger panel is a more efficient one (232 W/m2 against 205 W/m2). But area is rarely the real constraint; shape is. A 2.28 m module will not cross a camper roof between the vents where a 1.72 m one will. Panel buying is a rectangle-packing problem wearing an energy costume. (2) WEIGHT AND HANDLING. About 21.5 kg against 28.5 kg, and a glass-glass bifacial 600 W reaches 35 kg. One person places the first; the second is a two-person lift before a ladder is involved, and 2.58 m2 of sail area in a gust is a risk no datasheet mentions. Explicitly NOT a structural argument -- per square metre the two builds weigh the same, so this is about the install day, not the roof. (3) ARRANGEMENT GRANULARITY. panels = series x parallel, so the count you land on decides how many arrangements exist at all. 3200 W of 400 W panels is 8, which divides four ways; of 600 W panels it is 5 or 6, and FIVE IS PRIME -- all in series or all in parallel, nothing between, and if neither fits your window the only move is a sixth panel you did not want. Same class of conflict system-design.ts already reports, where every odd panel count had no safe arrangement for one real pairing. (4) CURRENT PER STRING IS SET BY THE PANEL, NOT THE ARRAY -- the sharpest difference, and the one a first pass at this analysis got WRONG and had to correct. Current limits are PER TRACKER, so two parallel strings on a two-tracker unit is one string each; the naive "28 A exceeds 25 A" comparison was wrong. The honest version uses the EG4 6000XP pair, 17 A usable against 25 A short-circuit, and the code''s own PV_IRRADIANCE_FACTOR of 1.25: the 600 W example presents 17.38 A and the 400 W one 14.38 A, so the large panel is OVER the harvest limit and clips while both stay well under the damage limit. Same watts on paper, one of them quietly throwing away its peaks. A capacity-register finding, not a protection one. (5) SHADE AND GRACEFUL FAILURE. One panel of two is up to half a string, one of three is a third, and the same holds for a cracked panel or a corroded connector. Caveated honestly: bypass diodes and half-cut layouts make the real fractions kinder than the arithmetic, and where shade is serious the true fix is a separate tracker, not a smaller panel. (6) COST IS PER WATT PLUS PER PANEL PLUS FREIGHT. Per watt the big panel wins; per count (rails, clamps, roof penetrations, MC4 pairs, labour) the small one does. FREIGHT is the ambush -- past about 2 m a panel stops being a parcel and ships LTL on a pallet, often kerbside with a lift-gate fee, which on a two-to-six panel DIY order can exceed everything the bigger format saved. Compare delivered totals, never per-watt prices. (7) THE SPEC THAT OUTLIVES THE CHOICE. p-type PERC against n-type TOPCon: about -0.35 against -0.29 %/degC, 2% against 1% first-year degradation, 0.55 against 0.4 %/yr after. Compounded over twenty-five years that dwarfs the 400-vs-600 decision entirely. AND IT UNLOCKED A TEACHING MOMENT the site had been missing: that same coefficient is ALREADY a protection input, because it governs cold-morning Voc rise and therefore how many panels may go in series. One number, two consequences -- what you harvest for twenty-five years, and how you are allowed to wire the array on day one. The guide hands that half to /guides/strings-and-mppt rather than repeating it. (8) MIXING AND EXPANSION. A series string runs at its weakest panel''s current, and flagship large-format models churn fast, so a stage-built array argues for the commoner 400-450 W format. REGISTER: nearly all of this is capacity and practical, so it is allowed to be specific and opinionated under the capacity/protection split. The two protection-shaped parts -- cold Voc setting series count, and per-tracker current -- state the mechanism and hand off to /calculators/strings rather than emitting a number. Guides teach. WHAT SHIPPED WITH IT: EXAMPLE_PANEL_LARGE in src/lib/pv-string.ts, a 600 W sibling of EXAMPLE_PANEL on exactly the same terms -- deliberately nobody''s product, round numbers, and it passes every panel-review.ts check (Vmp x Imp = 601.9 W against a 600 W nameplate, fill factor 0.79). Three tests lock it: that both worked examples raise no review flags, that the large one carries the GENTLER Voc coefficient (load-bearing for the section 7 argument, so a later tidy-up cannot quietly invert it), and that the 600 W string clips a 17 A tracker where the 400 W one does not. The guide computes every electrical figure live from pv-string.ts so it cannot drift from the calculators. ITS PHYSICAL FIGURES CANNOT DO THE SAME, and that is the sibling item: PanelSpec carries no dimensions, so the size and weight table is hand-written, class-typical, and labelled as such on the page.',
   'in_test', 90, true, 101),
  (1, 'calculators', 'Panel choice: form factor, granularity and the physical fields',
   'THE FINDING THAT MOTIVATES THIS, from the 2026-09-10 panel-guidance session: PanelSpec in src/lib/pv-string.ts has TEN FIELDS AND EVERY ONE OF THEM IS A VOLT OR AN AMP. No length, no width, no mass, no module efficiency, no cell technology, no degradation rate. That is precisely why the site could not answer "why 3 x 400 W rather than 2 x 600 W" until /guides/choosing-panels hand-wrote the physical numbers as class-typical prose with a caveat attached. Widening the model is what makes the whole question computable instead of editorial. FIELDS TO ADD: lengthMm, widthMm, weightKg, moduleEfficiency, cellTech (perc | topcon | hjt), bifacial, degradationYear1, degradationAnnual, warrantyYears, connector. A FOURTH CROSS-CHECK COMES FREE in panel-review.ts, in the same physics-shaped family as the existing fill factor test: module efficiency must equal Pmax / (area x 1000 W/m2), which catches dimensions read off the wrong row without needing any new source of truth. Weight per square metre is a second: glass-backsheet lands near 11-13 kg/m2, glass-glass near 15-17. WHAT IT UNLOCKS, in value order. (1) An ARRANGEMENT-GRANULARITY output: for a chosen tracker and energy target, show WHICH PANEL WATTAGES have valid whole-string arrangements. Highest value per hour on the board and mostly assembly -- evaluateArrangements() and system-design.ts already do the work -- and it answers the guide''s question against the user''s OWN inverter rather than a worked example. (2) A FORM-FACTOR AND HANDLING panel on /calculators/panels: array area, per-panel weight, a one-person-lift flag, total footprint, and longest dimension against a stated mounting limit. Pairs directly with the Phase 1 item "Ask installation environment up front" -- cabin / boat / camper / shed IS the input that decides form factor, and neither item is worth as much without the other. (3) A SIDE-BY-SIDE BUILD COMPARISON (3 x 400 against 2 x 600) showing area, weight, valid arrangements, shade exposure and delivered cost. Also the natural affiliate surface, since panels are the largest line item after the battery. (4) A DERIVED FREIGHT-CLASS field: longest dimension over about 2 m means LTL rather than parcel. Cheap to compute, deliberately NOT scraped, and real DIY decision information nobody else surfaces. SEQUENCING, decided 2026-09-10: this comes AFTER the battery pipeline''s scheduled re-scrape lands. Items 1 to 3 above need no new table at all and are pure differentiator #1, so the calculator half can proceed immediately; opening panel_models first would mean two unfinished pipelines at once. Ship one pipeline before starting the second.',
   'planned', 0, true, 102);
